fact WorkItem(id: "WI-362", description: some(value: "Proposal docs/proposals/library/002-iteration-collection.md Phase 1 tail + the stdlib half of its soundness gate. Two gaps remain in stdlib/anthill/prelude/stream.anthill. (1) Stream does NOT provide Iterable. The proposal (Relationship to Stream) says Stream trivially provides Iterable: iterator(s) = s. Add fact Iterable[C = Stream, Element = T, E] + operation iterator(s: Stream) -> Stream = s. This is the only carrier-side Phase-1 gap left (List already provides Iterable at list.anthill:65; the only fact Iterable in stdlib is List). (2) Stream.takeN and Stream.collect are DECLARED (signature + effects E) but have NO definition anywhere -- neither a derivation rule nor an operation body (list.anthill:63 comment: declared on Stream but have no derivation rule yet). Stream has ONE primitive, splitFirst; head/tail/isEmpty are already derived from it via rule-laws, but takeN/collect are not. Consequence: fact Stream[List] (list.anthill:64) is trusted-not-true -- collect(xs)/takeN(xs,n) over a List resolve to nothing. Give both a default definition over splitFirst in the OPERATION-BODY shape (operation takeN(s,n) -> List = match splitFirst(s) ...; operation collect(s) -> List = match splitFirst(s) ...), NOT new rules -- per the typeclass-satisfaction-is-operations-not-rules convention (the existing head/tail/isEmpty rule-laws are WI-324 rule->operation cleanup, out of scope here). Result: every fact Stream[X] carrier (List, LogicalStream) backs the full Stream interface from splitFirst alone, finishing proposal-002 Phase 1. PURE STDLIB -- no compiler/typer change (WI-344 provider-admissibility already landed, so iterator(s: Stream) -> Stream = s and fact Iterable typecheck). REF: stdlib/anthill/prelude/{stream,iterable,list}.anthill. ACCEPTANCE: stream.anthill + list.anthill load; fact Iterable[C=Stream] resolves; a test evaluating collect and takeN over a List consumed as a Stream returns the expected List; full anthill-core green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-365"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-03T08:19:26Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-362", author: "user", content: "POSTPONED behind WI-365 (typer facility), per user direction. Investigation this session: PART 1 (Stream provides Iterable) IS pure stdlib and verified GREEN -- adding fact Iterable[C = Stream, Element = T] + operation iterator(s: Stream) -> Stream = s to stream.anthill (incl. a stream<->iterable cyclic import, which the 2-pass loader accepts) loads cleanly (eval test wi362_stream_provides_iterable_loads passed). PART 2 (collect/takeN default bodies) is NOT pure stdlib -- its premise was false. A default operation body on the ABSTRACT Stream sort does not typecheck: `s: Stream` binds Stream[T=?_,E=?_] not Stream[T=T,E=E], so splitFirst(s) returns Option[Pair[A=?_]]@?_ -> `match.rule: expected List[T=T], got List[T=?_]` (element) + `collect.effects: expected [E], got undeclared effect: ?_` (effect). WI-357 bind_spec_params_from_carrier only fires for a CONCRETE carrier, not the abstract self-receiver. Same wall blocks consuming List-as-Stream for head/collect/takeN (missing requires Stream + effect cannot ground to {}). Filed WI-365 with the detailed root-cause + fix direction (def-side dual of WI-357: bind a spec ops params+effect from the ENCLOSING sort). WI-362 now depends_on WI-365; its trip-wire is the (un-ignore-later) eval test wi362_collect_over_list_when_typer_grounds_effect_and_element. Working tree reverted to clean -- WI-362 will be implemented as one unit (Iterable + collect/takeN bodies) AFTER WI-365.", at: "2026-06-02T10:12:27Z")
